Day 66/100 of #DrGViswanathanChallenge

Continuing my DSA journey with Binary Trees

Today, I solved LeetCode 662 – Maximum Width of Binary Tree.

- Approach:

I used BFS (Level Order Traversal) + Indexing to calculate the width of each level.

Assign an index to every node as if the tree were a complete binary tree.

For a node at index i:

Left child → 2 * i

Right child → 2 * i + 1

Use a queue to process the tree level by level.

For each level, record the index of the first and last node.

The width of that level is:

lastIndex - firstIndex + 1

Keep track of the maximum width across all levels.

This indexing technique allows the null gaps between nodes to be counted correctly.



-Time Complexity: O(N) 

- Space Complexity: O(N)



-Key Takeaways

1) Practiced BFS / Level Order Traversal

2) Learned how indexing can represent positions in a binary tree

3) Understood how to count gaps between non-null nodes



